00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:31.640
And then my team, we took a
bunch of games based on GCP,

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:34.900
so Cloud Functions, BigQuery,
all these GCP technologies,

00:09:34.900 --> 00:09:36.800
and then ported them to
the Instruct platform

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:38.967
so then attendees could
play them on the show floor.

00:09:38.967 --> 00:09:40.675
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Which was a lot of fun,

00:09:40.675 --> 00:09:42.932
and I think that we saw a
tremendous amount of people

00:09:42.932 --> 00:09:45.140
at Next kind of hanging
around those arcade machines.

00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:47.598
Just like you said, I want to
get back to how they were set

00:09:47.598 --> 00:09:50.042
up at Next, but I guess,
Adé, the question is,

00:09:50.042 --> 00:09:51.500
who thought of the
arcade machines?

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:52.460
How did that come about?

00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:53.060
ADÉ MOCHTAR: Yeah, yeah.

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:54.810
So that was actually
one of our developers

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:57.800
that had some sort of a
brainwave where we thought,

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:00.410
hey, we need to do something
different at conferences.

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:02.840
You can quite easily get
a booth, and get a stand,

00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:05.440
and hang out, and give
people stickers or something.

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:07.440
But we thought, let's
do something different.

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:09.365
Let's bring an arcade
machine to a conference.

00:10:09.365 --> 00:10:10.740
And then we got
started thinking,

00:10:10.740 --> 00:10:12.615
so what do we need to
do to get this working,

00:10:12.615 --> 00:10:13.980
get some traction?

00:10:13.980 --> 00:10:15.860
We have a house designer.

00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:17.120
We pitched the idea to him.

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:19.058
He basically said,
this is a great idea.

00:10:19.058 --> 00:10:20.600
I'm going to build
a machine for you.

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:23.330
It's a custom build, so I'll
build the physical thing,

00:10:23.330 --> 00:10:25.850
and you guys have to build
the software around it.

00:10:25.850 --> 00:10:27.070
That's exactly what we did.

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:28.903
And we brought it to a
different conference,

00:10:28.903 --> 00:10:31.920
and basically we put it there,
and the attraction was amazing.

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:34.380
So everybody wants to see
what this thing is about.

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:35.890
Basically, our
target audience, they

00:10:35.890 --> 00:10:37.670
grew up in the '70s
and '80s, right?

00:10:37.670 --> 00:10:39.660
And everybody knows
arcade machines.

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:41.900
Everybody has
played arcade games,

00:10:41.900 --> 00:10:43.140
and everybody loves them.

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:45.770
So the moment people
see an arcade machine,

00:10:45.770 --> 00:10:47.020
they're instantly drawn to it.

00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:48.895
But then you walk up to
it, and you say, hey,

00:10:48.895 --> 00:10:51.440
there's something different
about this arcade cabinet.

00:10:51.440 --> 00:10:52.460
This one has a keyboard.

00:10:52.460 --> 00:10:53.510
So what's going on?

00:10:53.510 --> 00:10:55.880
Now people approach
it, they push start,

00:10:55.880 --> 00:10:58.490
and they see this
different kind of content--

00:10:58.490 --> 00:11:00.560
more serious games,
but still games.

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:01.708
People just love that.

00:11:01.708 --> 00:11:02.875
JON FOUST: I definitely did.

00:11:02.875 --> 00:11:05.900
I actually had access to the
showcase floor prior to Next,

00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:08.657
and the first thing I saw
when I came down the escalator

00:11:08.657 --> 00:11:09.740
was those arcade machines.

00:11:09.740 --> 00:11:11.840
And I definitely walked
up, that's where I met Adé

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:15.140
for the first time, and I just
hung around for maybe two hours

00:11:15.140 --> 00:11:15.920
or so.

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:18.410
And just to let you know,
guys, I am that game guy,

00:11:18.410 --> 00:11:21.470
so you guys definitely caught
my attention immediately as soon

00:11:21.470 --> 00:11:22.970
as I showed up.

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:25.220
But my question
is, although you've

00:11:25.220 --> 00:11:28.720
been enticing a lot of people by
creating these arcade machines

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:31.090
and drawing them
in, can you tell us

00:11:31.090 --> 00:11:35.080
almost like the motivation as
to why you use the machines?

00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:37.150
I know that we wanted
to entice people,

00:11:37.150 --> 00:11:38.390
we wanted to add a keyboard.

00:11:38.390 --> 00:11:40.865
But what really draws
people to continue

00:11:40.865 --> 00:11:43.240
to keep playing that games
once they get on the keyboard?

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:44.700
Because it is a
different type of game.

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:46.783
ADÉ MOCHTAR: Yeah, it is
a different type of game.

00:11:46.783 --> 00:11:49.300
But we do use gamification
elements like a leaderboard,

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:52.108
like different challenges,
like a validation step

00:11:52.108 --> 00:11:54.400
where you actually get to
see whether what you're doing

00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:55.120
is correct.

00:11:55.120 --> 00:11:57.450
And all those elements,
they make people come back

00:11:57.450 --> 00:12:00.190
to the arcade games, try to
beat their own high scores,

00:12:00.190 --> 00:12:01.660
try to top the leaderboard.

00:12:01.660 --> 00:12:03.820
So those are all elements
that really draw people

00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:04.825
to these machines.

00:12:04.825 --> 00:12:05.710
SANDEEP DINESH: Something
I can to add to that.

00:12:05.710 --> 00:12:07.540
At Next one of the
really cool things that

00:12:07.540 --> 00:12:10.860
happened was people came
back multiple times to try

00:12:10.860 --> 00:12:12.997
to beat each other's
scores, and kept, like,

00:12:12.997 --> 00:12:14.080
I can get higher than you.

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:15.177
I can get higher than you.

00:12:15.177 --> 00:12:17.260
And so the leaderboards
just kept going up and up,

00:12:17.260 --> 00:12:19.250
and people were really
excited about that.

00:12:19.250 --> 00:12:20.333
JON FOUST: That's awesome.

00:12:20.333 --> 00:12:21.850
So can you tell us
a little bit more

00:12:21.850 --> 00:12:26.320
about why you decided to gamify
things, like learning by doing,

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:28.480
I guess, would be like the
best definition for it.

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:31.450
ADÉ MOCHTAR: So the idea
originated in a conference that

00:12:31.450 --> 00:12:33.820
we got organized
together with HashiCorp.

00:12:33.820 --> 00:12:36.700
So they organized the first
EU conference in Amsterdam,

00:12:36.700 --> 00:12:38.437
and we were co-organizing.

00:12:38.437 --> 00:12:40.520
And we thought, hey, let's
do something different.

00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:42.880
We need to get the
tools of HashiCorp

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:44.990
into the hands of their users.

00:12:44.990 --> 00:12:47.260
So we created a little bit
of a competition platform,

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:48.910
very similar to
what you currently

00:12:48.910 --> 00:12:51.700
see on the arcade machines,
or on Instruct.com,

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:53.290
but we gave them
sandbox environments

00:12:53.290 --> 00:12:55.720
with some challenges
around their form,

00:12:55.720 --> 00:12:57.050
console, [INAUDIBLE].

00:12:57.050 --> 00:12:58.870
And we asked them to solve it.

00:12:58.870 --> 00:13:01.390
Purely to focus was a
competition platform.

00:13:01.390 --> 00:13:03.497
And it was such a big
success that people even

00:13:03.497 --> 00:13:05.080
went back to their
hotel because there

00:13:05.080 --> 00:13:07.380
was better Wi-Fi to actually
solve the challenges.

00:13:07.380 --> 00:13:09.130
But a lot of the
feedback that we also got

00:13:09.130 --> 00:13:11.200
is this is a great way
to learn new technology.

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:12.540
Because a lot of
people, they maybe

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:14.415
touched one of these
tools, but not all four.

00:13:14.415 --> 00:13:17.020
And we got people into
contact with different tools

00:13:17.020 --> 00:13:20.720
that they never knew about, and
they learned a lot about it.

00:13:20.720 --> 00:13:22.730
So that feedback
got us thinking.

00:13:22.730 --> 00:13:26.050
So this is maybe a very good
way to learn new technology,

00:13:26.050 --> 00:13:29.560
and make it into a learning
platform rather than just

00:13:29.560 --> 00:13:30.518
a competition platform.

00:13:30.518 --> 00:13:31.893
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
So on one side,

00:13:31.893 --> 00:13:33.700
you've got the
arcade machines now,

00:13:33.700 --> 00:13:35.690
which it sounds like
are pretty popular way.

00:13:35.690 --> 00:13:36.190
Right?

00:13:36.190 --> 00:13:38.890
I mean, any arcade machine
that doesn't require quarters,

00:13:38.890 --> 00:13:41.690
I think, will get a pretty
big audience just by itself.

00:13:41.690 --> 00:13:42.340
ADÉ MOCHTAR: [CHUCKLES] Yeah.

00:13:42.340 --> 00:13:43.715
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
But you've also

00:13:43.715 --> 00:13:45.470
combined it with
these online labs

00:13:45.470 --> 00:13:47.410
and these different experiences.

00:13:47.410 --> 00:13:50.080
How does the platform
where you actually setup

00:13:50.080 --> 00:13:51.747
those labs-- how is
that actually built?

00:13:51.747 --> 00:13:53.663
ADÉ MOCHTAR: So we build
on top of Kubernetes.

00:13:53.663 --> 00:13:55.460
We use a lot of goaling
in our back end,

00:13:55.460 --> 00:13:58.420
we use React for the front end,
and we use a lot of Terraform

00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:00.480
to basically make
the platform happen.

00:14:00.480 --> 00:14:02.320
That, in combination
with some Bash here

00:14:02.320 --> 00:14:04.480
and there, maybe a
little bit too much,

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:07.420
but that's how the
internet runs, right?

00:14:07.420 --> 00:14:10.040
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Hey, Bash
is the glue of the internet.

00:14:10.040 --> 00:14:10.900
ADÉ MOCHTAR: Yeah, definitely.

00:14:10.900 --> 00:14:11.970
JON FOUST: Don't bash on Bash.

00:14:11.970 --> 00:14:13.720
ADÉ MOCHTAR: No, I
seriously love Bash.

00:14:13.720 --> 00:14:16.060
If it weren't for Bash, I
would be lost, probably.

00:14:16.060 --> 00:14:18.520
But as we're still a small
team, we're currently

00:14:18.520 --> 00:14:22.150
with five people, we try to
use as many managed services as

00:14:22.150 --> 00:14:22.690
possible.

00:14:22.690 --> 00:14:25.270
So for Kubernetes, we use GKE.

00:14:25.270 --> 00:14:26.980
For our database we
use Cloud Sequel.

00:14:26.980 --> 00:14:29.110
For our build environments,
we use Cloud Build.

00:14:29.110 --> 00:14:31.610
For authentication,
Firebase authentication.

00:14:31.610 --> 00:14:34.120
So as many managed
services as possible,

00:14:34.120 --> 00:14:36.670
so we can focus on things that
are important for us, as how

00:14:36.670 --> 00:14:39.360
to spin up the sandboxes, how
to present it to the users,

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:40.450
all stuff like that.

00:14:40.450 --> 00:14:42.158
MARK MIRCHANDANI: It's
not the first time

00:14:42.158 --> 00:14:44.050
that we've kind of
seen a company try

00:14:44.050 --> 00:14:45.970
to teach all the
technologies, and they

00:14:45.970 --> 00:14:48.760
found that the best way
to teach it is also to run

00:14:48.760 --> 00:14:50.240
on those same technologies.

00:14:50.240 --> 00:14:52.320
So it's always super
interesting to hear about.

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:53.290
Yeah, you know, I
mean, what's the best

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:55.840
way to teach Kubernetes is
to get the managed Kubernetes

00:14:55.840 --> 00:14:58.330
environment, and then you
run your info on there too.

00:14:58.330 --> 00:14:59.200
ADÉ MOCHTAR: Yeah, definitely.

00:14:59.200 --> 00:15:00.650
I think if you want
to start teaching,

00:15:00.650 --> 00:15:01.630
you have to learn first.

00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:03.340
So you have to learn
about this technology.

00:15:03.340 --> 00:15:05.190
And once you get a little
bit of a grip on it,

00:15:05.190 --> 00:15:06.898
then you can start
teaching other people.

00:15:06.898 --> 00:15:09.170
Then you really start to
learn about this technology.

00:15:09.170 --> 00:15:10.610
The best way to
learn, in my opinion,

00:15:10.610 --> 00:15:12.110
is to teach others,
because you have

00:15:12.110 --> 00:15:14.313
to go into a much
greater level of detail

00:15:14.313 --> 00:15:15.730
to understand what
you're actually

00:15:15.730 --> 00:15:18.152
doing to be able to explain
it to different people.

00:15:18.152 --> 00:15:19.360
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Absolutely.

00:15:19.360 --> 00:15:22.390
So Sandeep, how did that
translate into the work

00:15:22.390 --> 00:15:25.150
that you kind of led, and
some of the other developer

00:15:25.150 --> 00:15:27.857
advocates here, and developer
program engineers worked on?

00:15:27.857 --> 00:15:29.440
SANDEEP DINESH: Yeah,
so the challenge

00:15:29.440 --> 00:15:32.530
that we faced was how
to make traditionally,

00:15:32.530 --> 00:15:35.200
quote unquote, "boring,
enterprise platform"

00:15:35.200 --> 00:15:36.610
fun and exciting.

00:15:36.610 --> 00:15:40.030
And so Instruct kind of helps
with that by having these,

00:15:40.030 --> 00:15:42.050
like, checks as Adé calls them.

00:15:42.050 --> 00:15:44.650
So every step of the way,
the platform actually

00:15:44.650 --> 00:15:47.950
runs code on their side to check
that you actually completed

00:15:47.950 --> 00:15:49.520
whatever task was set for you.

00:15:49.520 --> 00:15:50.020
Right?

00:15:50.020 --> 00:15:52.203
So you can't just cheat
your way through the games.

00:15:52.203 --> 00:15:53.620
So that was really
powerful, being

00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:56.710
able to run code to check
what they did, spinning up

00:15:56.710 --> 00:15:59.080
their own sandbox, to
have an experiment,

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:00.560
and have a no guide rails.

00:16:00.560 --> 00:16:01.060
Right?

00:16:01.060 --> 00:16:03.370
So with that, with things
like our GKE challenge,

00:16:03.370 --> 00:16:05.920
they could either
use a Kubernetes CLI,

00:16:05.920 --> 00:16:07.450
they could write
it in a YAML file--

00:16:07.450 --> 00:16:10.490
the choice was theirs on
how to solve these problems.

00:16:10.490 --> 00:16:12.730
So that gave us as a
team a lot of flexibility

00:16:12.730 --> 00:16:13.490
in designing them.

00:16:13.490 --> 00:16:13.990
Right?

00:16:13.990 --> 00:16:15.760
We didn't have to be
like kind of forced

00:16:15.760 --> 00:16:17.232
into any sort of pattern.

00:16:17.232 --> 00:16:18.940
As long as it worked
on the command line,

00:16:18.940 --> 00:16:20.648
we could use it on
the Instruct platform.

00:16:20.648 --> 00:16:22.370
And I found that
really powerful.

00:16:22.370 --> 00:16:23.120
ADÉ MOCHTAR: Yeah.

00:16:23.120 --> 00:16:24.760
It's [INAUDIBLE]
stuff that you can do.

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:27.280
We also have an option
to run UIs, and maybe not

00:16:27.280 --> 00:16:29.190
as effectively on
the arcade machines,

00:16:29.190 --> 00:16:31.270
but our normal platform
does UIs, as well.

00:16:31.270 --> 00:16:34.920
It allows us to expose
basically any web app or even

00:16:34.920 --> 00:16:37.770
desktop sessions to users,
which makes it really powerful.

00:16:37.770 --> 00:16:40.020
SANDEEP DINESH: So we actually
used Instruct's ability

00:16:40.020 --> 00:16:43.230
to run arbitrary apps and
connect them to their back end

00:16:43.230 --> 00:16:46.500
to run a full RPG in the
Instruct arcade machine.

00:16:46.500 --> 00:16:49.530
So it was almost like
there was an actual video

00:16:49.530 --> 00:16:52.410
game running in the arcade
machines that taught you

00:16:52.410 --> 00:16:53.580
about coding.

00:16:53.580 --> 00:16:55.840
It was "Inception"
in many levels.

00:16:55.840 --> 00:16:56.640
MARK MIRCHANDANI: First
we're teaching people

00:16:56.640 --> 00:16:59.070
about the environment running
on that actual environment.

00:16:59.070 --> 00:17:01.290
Now we're basically just
modeling being real life.

00:17:01.290 --> 00:17:02.786
[CHUCKLES]

00:17:02.786 --> 00:17:04.619
JON FOUST: This is kind
of a funny question.

00:17:04.619 --> 00:17:06.510
Maybe Adé would answer this.

00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:09.002
So if I were at your arcade
machine and I just do up,

00:17:09.002 --> 00:17:10.710
up, down, down, left,
right, left, right,

00:17:10.710 --> 00:17:12.280
B, A, what would happen?

00:17:12.280 --> 00:17:12.829
[CHUCKLES]

00:17:12.829 --> 00:17:14.329
ADÉ MOCHTAR:
Unfortunately, nothing.

00:17:14.329 --> 00:17:14.829
No.

00:17:14.829 --> 00:17:15.990
[LAUGHING]

00:17:15.990 --> 00:17:18.109
It is something that
we thought about.

00:17:18.109 --> 00:17:20.849
I remember back-- I think it
was eight or nine years ago

00:17:20.849 --> 00:17:24.180
at my previous company, we did
have that code on our website.

00:17:24.180 --> 00:17:27.465
It would start showing My Little
Pony figures on the websites.

00:17:27.465 --> 00:17:29.840
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Oh, I remember
that JavaScript library.

00:17:29.840 --> 00:17:30.870
That was a fun one to add.

00:17:30.870 --> 00:17:31.070
ADÉ MOCHTAR: Yeah, yeah.

00:17:31.070 --> 00:17:31.808
Yeah.

00:17:31.808 --> 00:17:33.600
JON FOUST: We've talked
about the platform,

00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:36.030
and how it's built, and all
the technologies around it,

00:17:36.030 --> 00:17:38.520
but I'm curious, what other
technologies do you actually

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:39.645
support in these sandboxes?

00:17:39.645 --> 00:17:41.728
ADÉ MOCHTAR: So we support
with quite a wide range

00:17:41.728 --> 00:17:42.460
of technology.

00:17:42.460 --> 00:17:44.360
So in the basis we
can run containers

00:17:44.360 --> 00:17:45.720
and virtual machines.

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:49.350
But we also have the ability to
start complete new Google Cloud

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:51.190
projects, or an AWS account.

00:17:51.190 --> 00:17:53.670
And we're also looking at
adding different cloud vendors,

00:17:53.670 --> 00:17:55.110
as well, and we
can mix and match

00:17:55.110 --> 00:17:57.127
all these different
technologies.

00:17:57.127 --> 00:17:59.460
So that gives you a lot of
freedom, a lot of flexibility

00:17:59.460 --> 00:18:02.130
to create arbitrarily
complex environments

00:18:02.130 --> 00:18:04.140
to basically create an
environment to teach

00:18:04.140 --> 00:18:06.160
your users whatever you need.

00:18:06.160 --> 00:18:08.552
JON FOUST: And, I'm curious,
when you're actually

00:18:08.552 --> 00:18:10.260
working in technologies
in the sandboxes,

00:18:10.260 --> 00:18:11.850
how do you actually
create these sandboxes,

00:18:11.850 --> 00:18:13.500
and what kind of
challenges do you actually

00:18:13.500 --> 00:18:14.620
have when you're creating them?

00:18:14.620 --> 00:18:17.037
ADÉ MOCHTAR: So the definition
of the sandbox is it's just

00:18:17.037 --> 00:18:18.653
a simple YAML file
that we defined.

00:18:18.653 --> 00:18:20.320
So there you can
specify your containers

00:18:20.320 --> 00:18:22.073
or your virtual
machines or whatnot.

00:18:22.073 --> 00:18:24.240
And you can use standard
images from the Docker hub,

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:26.680
for instance, or from Google
Cloud's computer images.

00:18:26.680 --> 00:18:28.350
And then we have
a set of scripts

00:18:28.350 --> 00:18:30.835
that we can run on these
containers or virtual machines.

00:18:30.835 --> 00:18:32.460
Those are simple Bash
scripts, and they

00:18:32.460 --> 00:18:34.980
get executed on that
infrastructure in that sandbox

00:18:34.980 --> 00:18:36.310
that you defined.

00:18:36.310 --> 00:18:39.690
